    "response": "An all-male interview panel is a valid cause for concern, but how you interpret and act on it requires a careful, strategic approach. \n\nHere is an analysis of what this fact might indicate and practical steps on how to handle the situation.\n\n---\n\n### 1. What Does an All-Male Panel Signify?\n\n* **A Potential Breach of Policy:** Most public broadcasters have strict Diversity, Equity, and Inclusion (DEI) guidelines and public-sector recruitment codes. Many explicitly mandate diverse interview panels (e.g., requiring at least one woman or independent external panelist) to ensure balanced perspectives. An all-male panel suggests internal diversity checks were either overlooked, bypassed, or absent.\n* **Risk of Unconscious / Affinity Bias:** An all-male panel does not automatically prove deliberate or unlawful discrimination. However, homogeneous panels are widely recognized in HR and organizational psychology as susceptible to affinity bias (the tendency to favor candidates with similar backgrounds, communication styles, or career paths).\n* **Not Conclusive Proof on Its Own:** Hiring decisions often come down to specific strategic priorities. If the broadcaster is heavily pivoting toward digital transformation, the panel may defend their choice by pointing to the successful candidate's specific digital experience, regardless of total years in service.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Is It a Sign of Anything Else?\n\n* **Procedural Sluggishness vs. Avoidance:** Two weeks to provide written feedback after senior-level interviews is not unusual in large public institutions due to HR sign-off cycles. However, silence can also occur when a panel realizes a decision or process may face scrutiny and HR is reviewing how feedback is framed.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Recommended Steps\n\n#### Step 1: Check Internal Recruitment & Diversity Policies\n* Look up the broadcaster’s official recruitment code, hiring guidelines, and DEI policies (often publicly available or accessible on the staff intranet).\n* Check specifically whether there are rules regarding:\n  * Minimum diversity requirements for interview panels.\n  * Timelines and obligations for providing written feedback to shortlisted internal or external candidates.\n  * Right of appeal or review for recruitment decisions.\n\n#### Step 2: Send a Professional Follow-up for Feedback\nBefore making any formal allegations, obtain the written feedback so you have their official justification on the record.\n\n> **Example phrasing:**  \n> *\"Dear [HR Contact / Panel Chair], I am following up on my request sent on [Date] for written feedback regarding the News Editor selection process. As an experienced member of the newsroom and finalist for the role, constructive feedback on how the panel evaluated my experience against the role's criteria—particularly regarding the strategic priorities for the department—would be valuable for my professional development. I would appreciate an update on when I might expect this.\"*\n\n#### Step 3: Consult Your Union or Staff Representative\n* If you are a member of a trade union (e.g., NUJ, MEAA, CWA, or equivalent) or a staff association, contact a representative confidentially.\n* Unions frequently handle recruitment compliance issues and will know whether:\n  * All-male panels are an ongoing issue at the broadcaster.\n  * The selection panel breached collective agreements or HR standards.\n  * There is precedent for challenging the appointment or requesting a procedural audit.\n\n#### Step 4: Access Your Interview Records (If Necessary)\n* If written feedback is withheld or evasive, you may have legal or organizational rights (such as a Subject Access Request / Privacy Act request, or Freedom of Information request depending on your jurisdiction) to see the panel’s scoring sheets and interview notes related to your evaluation.\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\n**Should you make something of it?** Yes, but procedurally first, rather than accusatorially. \n\nBegin by confirming whether the panel composition violated the broadcaster's own rules, secure your written feedback, and speak with a union or legal advisor before deciding whether to raise a formal grievance or inquiry.\n",
